Q: NextJS Tailwind Global.css parsing failure - removed issue but still unable to parse A: <p>I ran into the same <strong>global.css parsing error</strong>. Tried reverting to a working backup and still had the same issues. Then I realised related to <strong>Turbopack</strong>, not <strong>Tailwind</strong> itself. I disabled <strong>Turbopack</strong> and switched back to the standard <strong>Next.js dev server</strong>.</p>
<h3><strong>Update <a href="/questions/tagged/package.json" class="s-tag post-tag" title="show questions tagged &#39;package.json&#39;" aria-label="show questions tagged &#39;package.json&#39;" rel="tag" aria-labelledby="tag-package.json-tooltip-container" data-tag-menu-origin="Unknown">package.json</a></strong></h3>
<p>Change:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;dev&quot;: &quot;next dev --turbopack -p 3000&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>To:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;dev&quot;: &quot;next dev -p 3000&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Make sure to use your correct port.</p>
<h3><strong>Next Delete:</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>.next</strong></p>
</li>
<li><p>and <strong>node_modules</strong> folders</p>
</li>
<li><p>and <strong>package-lock.json</strong> file</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Then run a new clean install reinstallation:</h3>
<pre><code>npm install
</code></pre>
<p>Just incase, I also added this to to my next.config file to stop any Turbopack related config from interfering:</p>
<pre><code>import type { NextConfig } from 'next';

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  turbopack: {
    resolveAlias: {},
  },
  webpack: (config) =&gt; {
    return config;
  },
};

export default nextConfig;
</code></pre>

