Q: C++ sizeof std::vector A: <p>&quot;If you have <code>std::vector&lt;int&gt; v(1000)</code> you will probably get the same size of 24&quot;.   No &quot;probably&quot; about it - it is a certainty.  The value of  <code>sizeof(v)</code> is equivalent to <code>sizeof(std::vector&lt;int&gt;)</code> , and is an implementation-defined value.     It has no relationship to <code>v.size()</code> .</p>
<p>The meaning of implementation-defined is, essentially, that the value is set (and documented) by the implementation.   So, for example, <code>sizeof(std::vector&lt;int&gt;)</code> may be 24 with one implementation (e.g. g++ for the OP) and may be a different value with a different implementation (e.g. a different compiler).</p>
<p>All operations of a <code>std::vector</code> that affect the size (as returned by <code>std::vector::size()</code>) do not change the size (given by the <code>sizeof</code>) of the vector.    So, in</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

int main()
{
    std::cout &lt;&lt; sizeof(std::vector&lt;int&gt;) &lt;&lt; '\n';
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v(1000);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; sizeof(v) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; '\n';
    v.resize(2000);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; sizeof(v) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; '\n';
}   
</code></pre>
<p>will produce output (assuming use of g++, like the OP) of the form</p>
<pre><code>24
24 1000
24 2000
</code></pre>
<p>i.e. the first values in the second and third line (24 in this case) will always be equal to the value output on the first line.      The output values of <code>v.size()</code>  (which depend on the state of <code>v</code> as determined at run time) will vary, while <code>sizeof(v)</code> will not.</p>
<p>In other words the <em>values</em> of the &quot;two pointers and size_t data members&quot; mention by the OP affect the value of <code>v.size()</code> but, since both pointers and <code>size_t</code> have a fixed (implementation-defined) size, the value of <code>sizeof(v)</code> does not change.</p>

Q: How do I make it so that the user only enters numbers in Console.ReadLine? A: <p>Use <code>decimal.TryParse()</code> instead of <code>Convert.ToDecimal()</code>, it'll return false on a bad input</p>
<pre><code>decimal valor;
while (true)
{
    Console.Write(&quot;Enter a number: &quot;);
    if (decimal.TryParse(Console.ReadLine(), out valor)) break;
    Console.WriteLine(&quot;Invalid input. Please enter a number.&quot;);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Difference between &quot;change&quot; and &quot;input&quot; event for an `input` element A: <p>It seems that this question has become one of the those questions that I visit from time to time. I'm not a fan of reading walls of text for simple things. So I decided to post a practical answer.</p>
<p>Using the following demo, one can examine which events are fired and in what order.</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/ArLpX.png" alt="screenshot of the demo" /></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let eventsToListen = [
    "focus",
    "blur",
    "input",
    "change",
];
let inputs = Array.from(
    document.querySelectorAll("#inputs :is(input, textarea, select)")
);
inputs.forEach(input =&gt; {
    input.eventQueue = [];
    let queueLimit = eventsToListen.length * 2;
    let queueDisplay = input.closest("td").nextElementSibling;
    eventsToListen.forEach(event =&gt; {
        input.addEventListener(event, () =&gt; {
            input.eventQueue.push(event);
            if (input.eventQueue.length &gt; queueLimit) {
                Array(input.eventQueue.length - queueLimit).fill(null).forEach(
                    _ =&gt; input.eventQueue.shift()
                );
            }
            queueDisplay.textContent = input.eventQueue.join(", ");
        });
    });
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>* {
